\enlargethispage{1\baselineskip}
\vspace{-6pt}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-2pt}

\looseness=-2 We presented ACR-VLA, an adaptive inference-time correction pipeline for frozen flow-matching VLAs. A $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ uncertainty probe ($\rho{=}0.9979$) gates a lightweight CRM (${\sim}$327K parameters). On SmolVLA (0.4B) across four LIBERO suites, unconditional CRM correction is unstable ($-$1.5\,pp Long, $-$1.3\,pp Object, $+$1.1\,pp Spatial), while ACR-VLA's adaptive gate provides improvement where the VLA is uncertain ($+$1.0--2.1\,pp on Long, Goal, and Spatial) and preserves baseline where it is confident (Object, 3.5\% trigger rate). The key finding is that adaptive gating---not correction strength---is the mechanism: the gate correctly allocates correction to uncertain steps while protecting confident ones.
\textbf{Limitations.}\enspace Effect sizes are $+$1.0--2.1\,pp on high-uncertainty suites (3 seeds for Spatial and Goal; 1 seed for Long). Seed-level differences are directionally positive but $n{=}3$ precludes statistical significance testing. On Object, ACR-VLA correctly preserves baseline ($\Delta{=}0.0$, 3 seeds) but the high variance ($\pm 3.0$\%) warrants additional evaluation. The pairwise $L_2$ uncertainty probe measures sample disagreement, not correctness; on Long (trigger rate 3.9\%), the VLA may produce confident but incorrect outputs that escape gating detection. Future work could combine disagreement-based and prediction-error-based uncertainty signals. The CRM requires task-specific calibration data and stochastic sample diversity---deterministic VLAs produce near-identical samples and lie outside scope (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}). Broader cross-VLA generalization and real-robot transfer remain future work. AI tools assisted with code and manuscript.

\appendix

\section{Applicability Boundary: Stochastic VLA Diversity}
\label{sec:applicability}

\looseness=-1 The multi-sample pipeline requires stochastic action diversity across samples. We validate this prerequisite on two VLA architectures: SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (flow matching, 0.4B) and OpenVLA-OFT~\cite{kim2024openvla} (deterministic $L_1$ regression, 7B).

SmolVLA produces meaningfully diverse action samples across all dimensions via its flow-matching generative process. In contrast, OpenVLA-OFT's deterministic $L_1$ regression produces near-identical outputs: mean pairwise $L_2{=}0.00436$ across $K{=}2$ samples, with $97.3$\% of steps below $0.01$. The uncertainty gate never fires, and multi-sample selection is vacuous.

This result establishes an architectural boundary: multi-sample inference-time correction is specific to stochastic generative VLAs (flow matching, diffusion). Deterministic VLAs lack the action diversity that selection and correction rely on. The boundary is architectural, not a limitation of our method: any inference-time approach that draws multiple action candidates requires the base policy to produce diverse samples.


\section{Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:tau_sweep}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Threshold sensitivity on Goal suite (seed 42, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$). VLA-only baseline: 83.5\% (seed-matched). Optimal performance at $\tau{=}0.15$; the selected operating point $\tau{=}0.25$ trades peak single-seed improvement for a more conservative trigger rate.}
\label{tab:tau_sweep}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$\tau$ & Trigger rate (\%) & SR (\%) & $\Delta$ vs VLA-only \\
\midrule
0.10 & 73.9 & 81.0 & $-2.5$ \\
0.15 & 33.9 & 85.0 & $+1.5$ \\
0.25 & 7--9 & 84.0 & $+0.5$ \\
0.40 & 1.2 & 83.5 & $0.0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:tau_sweep} reports success rates on Goal (seed 42) as a function of the uncertainty threshold $\tau$, with $\varepsilon{=}0.2$ (the adaptive correction magnitude). Performance follows an inverted-U pattern: at $\tau{=}0.10$ the gate triggers on 73.9\% of steps, overcorrecting confident ones and degrading performance by $-$2.5\,pp---reproducing the instability of unconditional correction. At $\tau{=}0.40$ the gate triggers on only 1.2\% of steps, effectively disabling the CRM and recovering VLA-only performance ($\Delta{=}0.0$).

\looseness=-1 The optimal operating region is $\tau \in [0.15, 0.25]$, where 8--34\% of steps receive correction and success rate improves by $+$0.5 to $+$1.5\,pp over the seed-matched baseline. The main experiments use $\tau{=}0.25$, which sacrifices peak single-seed improvement for a more conservative trigger rate. The inverted-U shape confirms that gating selectivity---not correction strength---drives performance: too much correction is as harmful as too little.

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Note on $\varepsilon$ comparisons.}\enspace The $\tau$-sweep uses $\varepsilon{=}0.2$ (the adaptive pipeline's norm bound). CRM always-on in Table~\ref{tab:main} uses a tighter $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ to limit overcorrection when applied unconditionally. The two settings are not directly comparable: CRM always-on ($\varepsilon{=}0.05$, 100\% trigger) achieves 85.5\% on Goal seed 42, exceeding $\tau{=}0.10$ ($\varepsilon{=}0.2$, 73.9\% trigger) at 81.0\%. This reflects the interaction between correction magnitude and trigger frequency---unconditional correction requires a gentler $\varepsilon$ to remain stable, while gated correction tolerates a larger $\varepsilon$ because it fires selectively.

