\subsection{Why Gating Is Necessary}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

\looseness=-1 The main results reveal a core finding: \emph{unconditional correction is unstable across suites}. CRM always-on applies the same learned residual to every step, including the ${\sim}$80--90\% where the VLA is already confident. This produces inconsistent effects: $+$1.1\,pp on Spatial, $\Delta{\approx}0$ on Goal, $-$1.3\,pp on Object (3 seeds), and $-$1.5\,pp on Long (1 seed). The Object and Long degradation shows that corrections on already-confident steps are harmful noise.

\looseness=-1 ACR-VLA's adaptive gate resolves this instability. On Object, where the VLA is already confident, the gate triggers on only 3.5\% of steps---the CRM barely fires, and the output matches baseline ($77.8$\%, $\Delta{=}0.0$, 3 seeds). This is the correct behavior: the gate identifies that Object needs no correction and avoids the $-$1.3\,pp harm of unconditional correction. On Spatial and Goal, where higher uncertainty triggers correction on 7--20\% of steps, the CRM provides targeted improvement ($+$2.1\,pp and $+$1.3\,pp respectively). On Long, the gate triggers on 3.9\% of steps; ACR-VLA improves by $+$1.0\,pp (1 seed) while CRM always-on degrades by $-$1.5\,pp.

The $K{=}10$ argmin baseline provides a complementary negative result: spending $10{\times}$ the inference budget on selection-only hurts on Goal ($-$2.9\,pp, 3 seeds) and Long ($-$1.5\,pp, 1 seed). Multi-sample selection refines the action but cannot fix systematic errors in the VLA's output distribution. Learned correction is necessary, but only when gated.

