\vspace{-6pt}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-2pt}

\looseness=-2 We presented ACR-VLA, an adaptive inference-time correction pipeline for frozen flow-matching VLAs. A $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ uncertainty probe ($\rho{=}0.9979$) gates a lightweight CRM (${\sim}$327K parameters). On SmolVLA (0.4B) across four LIBERO suites, unconditional CRM correction is unstable ($-$1.5\,pp Long, $-$1.3\,pp Object, $+$1.1\,pp Spatial), while ACR-VLA's adaptive gate provides improvement where the VLA is uncertain ($+$1.0--2.1\,pp on Long, Goal, and Spatial) and preserves baseline where it is confident (Object, 3.5\% trigger rate). The key finding is that adaptive gating---not correction strength---is the mechanism: the gate correctly allocates correction to uncertain steps while protecting confident ones.
\textbf{Limitations.}\enspace Effect sizes are $+$1.0--2.1\,pp on high-uncertainty suites (3 seeds for Spatial and Goal; 1 seed for Long). Seed-level differences are directionally positive but $n{=}3$ precludes statistical significance testing. On Object, ACR-VLA correctly preserves baseline ($\Delta{=}0.0$, 3 seeds) but the high variance ($\pm 3.0$\%) warrants additional evaluation. The pairwise $L_2$ uncertainty probe measures sample disagreement, not correctness; on Long (trigger rate 3.9\%), the VLA may produce confident but incorrect outputs that escape gating detection. Future work could combine disagreement-based and prediction-error-based uncertainty signals. The CRM requires task-specific calibration data and stochastic sample diversity---deterministic VLAs produce near-identical samples and lie outside scope (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}). Broader cross-VLA generalization and real-robot transfer remain future work. AI tools assisted with code and manuscript.

